  "text_md": "comet (t4). This is possible because the optimal flyby parameters (depth and inclination of the planetocentric hyperbola) can be computed directly from t2 and t4 . thus reducing the dimensionality of the problem.\n\n## 3.1.1 . Earth-GA segment\n\nFor a given flyby date; the ecliptic longitude of the GA planet is computed from the ephemeris as\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere nF is the mean motion of the planet . The minimum eccentricity of the conics that connect Earth   with the planet is determined by the condition of being able to reach the planet:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe upper bound of eccentricity is set by the maximum characteristic energy (C3) that the launcher is capable of:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere E denotes the mechanical energy and p is the gravitational parameter \\_ Due to the condition of departure from the perihelion; the eccentricity determines the transfer angle between Earth and the planet\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwith p = a(1 \\_ e2) denoting the semilatus rectum. Likewise; the transfer time (only the elliptic case is shown for the sake of brevity) can be computed from e t12\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->",
